"about": {
"title": "How role play works",
"html": "    <div class=\"home-emblem\"><svg class=\"il\"><use href=\"#il-mask\"/></svg></div>\n    <p class=\"lead\">Role play is not acting and not \"pretending\". It is permission. When you are strangers at a bar, you may say things you would never say in the first person, want things you are embarrassed to want on an ordinary day, and see your partner as if for the first time. The character is a thin mask, and what is underneath is exactly what gets revealed.</p>\n\n    <h2>Why it works</h2>\n    <p>Our minds get used to things. After years, the touch is familiar, the pace is known, and the ending can be guessed. A character breaks the habit in three places: <b>novelty</b> (\"someone else\" is touching you), <b>tension</b> (it is unclear what will happen, because the other has a secret goal), and <b>permission</b> (\"this is not me, so I may\"). The three together bring back the pulse of the beginning, without changing partners.</p>\n    <div class=\"pull\">The character is an excuse to tell the truth.</div>\n\n    <h2>How a scene is built here</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>The stage</b>: what the room pretends to be, which props, how long. Three items are enough for the body to believe: a light, a garment, an object.</li>\n      <li><b>A secret role card</b> for each of you: who the character is, how to play them, a <b>secret goal</b> the other does not know about, and an opening line. You hand over the phone, each reads only their own. The secret goals are the engine: they create a tug of war nobody has to stage.</li>\n      <li><b>Beats</b>: three beats that escalate. The first builds tension without touch, the second crosses one line, and the third is open: the scene continues wherever the two of you take it, in character. The app gives the frame, not the script.</li>\n      <li><b>Out of character</b>: every evening ends with a closing card: real names out loud, water, and three questions. It is not an extra, it is part of the scene.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>The initiator and the responder</h2>\n    <p>In every scene there is someone who opens (<b>the initiator</b>, {D} in the texts) and someone who responds ({S}). This is not \"strong and weak\": the responder almost always has a secret goal that flips the power halfway. The \"switches every scene\" mode swaps you, so that you both open and you both respond.</p>\n\n    <h2>Five rules of good players</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>\"Yes, and...\"</b> What the other offers exists. You do not argue with the facts of the scene, you add to them.</li>\n      <li><b>Stay in character even when you laugh.</b> Laughter is not failure. The character laughs, and you go on.</li>\n      <li><b>A new name out loud.</b> The small thing that most helps the body believe. Say it at the start of the scene and stop using real names until the end.</li>\n      <li><b>Slow.</b> The good scene is the one where the first beat is too long. The tension does the work, not the action.</li>\n      <li><b>The stop word is outside the character.</b> The character may say \"no\" as part of the game only if you agreed on that in advance, in words. The real stop word ends the scene in a second, without discussion.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>The categories here</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Strangers and seduction</b>: the easy entrance. Meeting again from zero.</li>\n      <li><b>Work and power</b>, <b>Service and clients</b>, <b>Authority and uniforms</b>, <b>Examination and care</b>: characters with a clear role, where the power comes from the situation and not the personality. Easy to step into because the script is already written in life.</li>\n      <li><b>Fantasy and costumes</b>: far from reality, and therefore especially freeing.</li>\n      <li><b>Forbidden</b>: the thrill of \"we are not supposed to\". Neighbors, exes, the wrong room.</li>\n      <li><b>Power play</b>: the characters agree on a contract in advance. Here it is especially important to read the category's safety note.</li>\n      <li><b>Identity swap</b>, <b>No words</b>, <b>Spies and mystery</b>, <b>Art and body</b>, <b>Bets and games</b>, <b>From afar and by phone</b>, <b>Another time</b>: the unconventional categories. Each breaks a different habit: identity, speech, distance or time. If the classics are already familiar, start here.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h2>Your own scenes</h2>\n    <p>The built-in scenes are frames. The intimate detail of the last beat is yours to write, in life or in the editor: \"All scenes\" → \"+ New\" gives you a card of your own with your own text, in both versions (who initiates), and you can attach a photo or video to it from the prompts list.</p>\n    <div class=\"pull\">Whoever dared to be someone else for an hour comes back a little more themselves.</div>\n\n    <h2>Where the name comes from</h2>\n    <p>Masks: in Greek theater the actor held a mask in front of the face, and the audience knew that what was said behind it was the truth of the character. Here the mask is thin, and what is behind it is yours.</p>\n"
},
